[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128379,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hybrid nerve sheath tumours are benign peripheral nerve sheath tumours with combined features of more than one conventional type (neurofibroma, schwannoma, perineurioma).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128380,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9563/0 Hybrid nerve sheath tumours</p>\r\n<p>9563/0 Perineurioma/schwannoma</p>\r\n<p>9563/0 Schwannoma/neurofibroma</p>\r\n<p>9563/0 Perineurioma/neurofibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128381,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F24 &amp; XH01G0 Benign cutaneous neoplasms of neural or nerve sheath origin &amp; Hybrid nerve sheath tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128382,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> benign nerve sheath tumour NOS.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128383,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Perineurioma/schwannoma; schwannoma/neurofibroma; perineurioma/neurofibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128384,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours show a wide anatomical distribution, most commonly occurring in the dermis or subcutaneous tissue {19623031; 9639114}. The most commonly reported site for hybrid schwannoma/perineurioma is on the fingers {15322875}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128385,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hybrid nerve sheath tumours manifest most often as painless masses in subcutaneous tissue or dermis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128386,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hybrid nerve sheath tumours are rare. The most common examples are tumours showing hybrid features of schwannoma and perineurioma, followed by hybrid neurofibroma/schwannoma. These tumours may occur at any age, with a peak in young adults and an equal sex distribution {19623031}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128387,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hybrid schwannoma/perineurioma occurs sporadically {19623031}, whereas hybrid neurofibroma/schwannoma is strongly associated with the neurofibromatoses {22446939}. A high prevalence of hybrid schwannoma/neurofibroma morphology was found in tumours from patients with schwannomatosis (71%), often leading to misdiagnosis as neurofibroma {22446939; 15955931}. Hybrid neurofibroma/perineurioma has been described in association with neurofibromatosis type&nbsp;1 {24751814; 23676318}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128388,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>VGLL3</em> gene rearrangement was found in most cases of hybrid perineurioma/schwannoma in one cohort, suggesting these tumours represent a distinct pathological entity {33649458}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128389,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, the tumours are well circumscribed, with a firm cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128390,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hybrid schwannoma/perineurioma shows a storiform or fascicular growth and is usually composed of an intimate admixture of alternating Schwann cells with plump nuclei and eosinophilic cytoplasm and perineurial cells with slender nuclei and delicate elongated cytoplasmic processes. The Schwann cell component is often prominent. The tumours may show degenerative nuclear atypia (ancient change). Mitoses are rare. The two components can be highlighted by immunohistochemistry: Schwann cells are positive for S100, and perineurial cells for EMA, claudin-1, and GLUT1 {19623031}.</p>\r\n<p>Hybrid neurofibroma/schwannoma is composed of schwannomatous nodules or Schwann cell bundles within an otherwise typical neurofibroma. Immunostaining highlights the mixed population of cells in the neurofibromatous component, including fibroblasts (CD34), Schwann cells (S100 and SOX10), and perineurial cells (EMA, claudin-1, and GLUT1), and the monomorphic Schwann cell populations in the Schwann cell solid nodules (highlighted by diffuse expression of S100 and SOX10) {9639114; 22446939}.</p>\r\n<p>Hybrid neurofibroma/perineurioma contains a plexiform neurofibroma with areas of perineurial differentiation. These areas are often only recognizable with the aid of immunohistochemistry. The perineuriomatous areas are immunopositive for EMA, GLUT1, and claudin-1, and the neurofibroma areas show a mixed population of cells including fibroblasts (CD34), Schwann cells (S100 and SOX10), and perineurial cells (EMA, claudin-1, and GLUT1) {23676318; 24461704}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128391,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 315,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:53:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
